course: adult-life
lesson: 24
title: When someone dies
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every living thing dies one day. When a person dies, their body stops working for ever; it is not sleep, and they do not come back.
explain: Grown-ups often use soft words for it, like "passed away", "we lost him" or "she's gone", because the plain word is hard to say.
explain: Picture a living room in Melbourne: Dad sits you down and says, "Grandad is dying. The doctors say it's weeks, not months." Later he cries, and it is the first time you have seen him cry.
explain: The sadness after a death is called grief. It comes and goes like waves, and people show it in many ways, from tears to casseroles on the doorstep.
explain: Different families believe different things about what happens after death, and nobody knows for certain.
fact: When doctors say someone is dying, the illness cannot be cured and the body is slowly stopping; they can often guess roughly how long is left.
fact: "Passed away" is a gentle way of saying "died"; underneath, it means the person has died and will not come back.
fact: Being dead means the body has stopped working for ever; it is not the same as sleep.
fact: A hospice is a calm place, a bit like a small hospital, that cares for people near the end of their lives; the aim is comfort and no pain, not a cure.
fact: Grown-ups cry when they are very sad, just like children; crying lets out big feelings and does not make them any less able to look after you.
fact: Grief, the sadness after someone dies, comes in waves: fine one day and terrible the next, sometimes set off by a song or a smell.
fact: Grief gets gentler over months and years; playing and laughing during that time does not mean you have forgotten the person.
fact: "At least it was peaceful" means the person died calmly, often without pain; people say it kindly, but "I'm sorry" is always a safer thing to say.
fact: A sudden death happens without warning, from an accident or a heart attack, so there is no chance to say goodbye; it is rare in young healthy people.
fact: When someone dies, neighbours and friends often bring food, so the family does not have to cook; it is a practical way of saying "we care".
fact: A widow is a woman whose husband has died, and a widower is a man whose wife has died.
fact: Different families and religions believe different things about death: some believe in heaven, some in rebirth, and some that life simply ends.
exercise: Dad says, "Grandad is dying. The doctors say it's weeks, not months." What does it mean? | His illness cannot be cured and he will probably die within weeks | He is very ill but will be completely better again in a few weeks' time | He is going on a long holiday for some months | The doctors are not sure he is ill at all
exercise: Grandad is dying. What can you still do? | Visit him, talk and hold his hand | Nothing, it is too late now for anything at all | Stay away so he can rest alone | Pretend everything is completely normal
exercise: Mum says quietly, "Mrs Jones next door passed away on Tuesday." What happened? | Mrs Jones died | Mrs Jones moved to a new house | Mrs Jones walked past on Tuesday | Mrs Jones went into hospital for a check
exercise: Why do grown-ups say "passed away" instead of "died"? | Softer words make sad news easier to say | It means something completely different from died | It is a rule that you must never say died | It means the person might still come back
exercise: Is being dead like being asleep? | No, the body has stopped working for ever | Yes, the person will wake up again in a while | Yes, but only for a very long time | No, being dead means you are just very tired
exercise: Mum on the phone: "They've moved Grandad to a hospice." What is a hospice? | A calm place caring for people near the end of life | A hospital where doctors do operations to cure people | A hotel where old people go on their holidays | A school where nurses go to learn their job
exercise: You see Dad cry for the first time. What is true? | Grown-ups cry when they are very sad, and he is still your dad | Dad is weak now and cannot look after you or anyone else any more | Grown-ups only cry when something hurts their body | You must make him stop crying straight away
exercise: What can you do when Dad is crying? | Sit with him or give him a hug | Tell him big men never cry | Leave the house until he stops | Make jokes until he laughs again
exercise: Mum says about her grief, "It comes in waves." What does she mean? | The sadness comes and goes, strong some days and gentle others | She feels sad only when she is at the seaside | She feels sad all the time, every hour, without any change at all | The sadness will be completely gone next week
exercise: A month after Grandma died, you laugh at a funny film. Does it mean you forgot her? | No, laughing and playing are normal while grieving | Yes, laughing means you did not really love her at all | Yes, you should not laugh for a whole year | No, but you must say sorry to everyone
exercise: A relative at the funeral says, "At least it was peaceful." What does it mean? | The person died calmly, without pain | The funeral was very quiet and boring | Everyone at the funeral got along well | The person was a very calm and quiet person
exercise: What is always a safe thing to say to someone whose relative has died? | "I'm sorry" | "At least he was old" | "Cheer up, it's fine" | "I know how you feel"
exercise: "It was very sudden. Nobody expected it." What kind of death is this? | One that happened without warning, like an accident or a heart attack | One that came after a long illness everyone in the family knew all about | One that happened very slowly over many years | One that the doctors had planned weeks before
exercise: Neighbours keep bringing round casseroles. Why? | So the grieving family does not have to cook | Because the neighbours cooked far too much food again | Because the family asked them for dinner | To get invited to the funeral afterwards
exercise: Gran says quietly, "I'm a widow now." What does she mean? | Her husband has died | She has moved to a new house | She has got married again | She has stopped working
exercise: What is a man called whose wife has died? | A widower | A widow | A bachelor | An orphan
exercise: You ask where Grandma is now and hear, "Some people believe she's in heaven." What is true? | Families believe different things, and nobody knows for certain | Everybody in the world believes exactly the same thing about it | Scientists have proved where dead people go | It is rude to ever ask about it
test: Your little brother says, "Grandad's just asleep, he'll wake up after the funeral." How do you put him right gently? | Grandad has died; his body has stopped and he will not wake up | Grandad is asleep and will wake up in a week or two | Grandad will wake up if we are good enough | Grandad is pretending and will come back
test: Mum on the phone: "We lost Bill on Sunday, it was his heart." What happened to Bill? | Bill died on Sunday of a heart problem | Bill got lost on a walk on Sunday | Bill lost his wallet with his heart-shaped key ring | Bill lost a race because of a stitch
test: Your cousin says, "Hospices are where they kill old people, innit." How do you put her right? | Hospices care for people near the end and keep them comfortable | She is right, it is a frightening place where nobody ever wants to go | Hospices are where old people learn to walk again | Hospices are only for very ill children
test: Dad, wiping his eyes: "Sorry, mate, don't mind me, I'm being soft." What is the kind answer? | "It's OK to cry, Dad" | "Stop it, you're embarrassing me" | "Grown-ups aren't allowed to cry" | "Go away until you've finished"
test: Six months on, Mum suddenly cries in the supermarket when Nan's favourite song comes on. Why? | Grief comes in waves and a song can bring it back | She is upset about the price of the shopping | She has hurt herself on the trolley | Six months on, she should be completely over it
test: At the wake, Auntie snaps, "Don't you 'at least' me, he was only sixty!" Why is she upset? | "At least" can feel like her sadness is being made smaller | She is cross that someone is wearing the wrong colour | She does not like the food at the wake | She thinks sixty is too young to retire
test: A boy at school says, "My dad died in a crash, no warning, nothing." What kind of death was it? | A sudden death | A peaceful death | An expected death | A death in a hospice
test: Your sister worries, "Uncle died suddenly, so Dad's gonna die suddenly too." What is true? | Sudden deaths are rare in healthy people, and she can ask a grown-up | She is right, sudden death runs in every family | Dad will certainly die soon too, because Uncle did and they are brothers | Nobody should ever talk about this with her
test: Dad laughs tiredly, "If one more lasagne comes through that door, we'll have to open a restaurant." Why is so much food arriving? | People bring food to a grieving family to show they care | Dad has started a business selling lasagne | The neighbours want to empty their own freezers | The family is having a big birthday party
test: On the news: "He leaves a widow and three children." What does this mean? | His wife and three children are still alive after his death | He has left his wife and children to live elsewhere with someone new | His wife has died and he has three children | He has given his house to three children
test: Your friend says, "My nan says Grandad's a star in the sky now, but my dad says he's just gone." Who is right? | People believe different things, and nobody knows for certain | The nan is right, because she is older | The dad is right, because men know more | Both are wrong, and the friend should not ask
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a hospice? | To provide comfort and relief from pain to persons nearing the end of life | To perform surgical operations with the intention of effecting a complete cure | To accommodate healthy elderly persons on holiday | To provide educational instruction to medical students
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the expression "passed away" when employed in respect of a person. | The person has died | The person has relocated | The person has walked past | The person has been admitted to hospital
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning grief. | It fluctuates over time and ordinarily diminishes over months and years | It remains constant and wholly unchanged for the remainder of a person's life | It ceases entirely upon the conclusion of the funeral | It is experienced exclusively by adults and never by children
exam: It is given that a woman's husband has died. Ascertain the term by which she is thereafter designated. | A widow | A widower | A spinster | A guardian
